tests:
  unit: { count: 8, covers: "The one production change this WU makes, asserted in both directions. TaskStep gains data-testid=task-<n>-state on the element that already wrapped its StatusLine - the direct successor to the retired step-trust-ca-status / step-set-proxy-status / step-verify-status ids the old spine read. The test pins the hook's presence WITH a state and its absence WITHOUT one, because the second half is the same contract: TaskStep renders no state where nothing observed one, and a hook that survived that emptiness would let an e2e assert the existence of a state the screen never claimed. Nothing else in the component moved - the number, the title, the purpose line, the dim rule and the slot are untouched, which is why the suite went 1137 -> 1138 rather than being rewritten. The full UI suite is green at the tip: 1138 Vitest tests in 55 files, beside the core workspace's untouched 754 (313 in trawler, 441 in xtask), with eslint and tsc clean. This WU changes no file under src/, xtask/ or tests/." }
  integration: { waived: "This WU adds no seam. The screen's seams against the API stub and a mocked invoke are W5's and are unchanged at this tip - App.test.tsx already drives armReach, disarmReach, the combined elevated action, the foreign-proxy confirm and the success banner, and none of those call sites moved. The only production byte this WU writes is a test hook on an element TaskStep already rendered, which has no seam to assert: it crosses no boundary, and a unit test of the component is the whole of its contract. Recorded as a waiver rather than as a count, because the honest statement is that the integration layer was not owed anything here, not that it was satisfied by tests written elsewhere." }
  e2e: { count: 34, covers: "THE DEBT THIS WU PAYS. W3, W4 and W5 each recorded the e2e as the epic's, and this is it: onboarding.spec.ts rewritten - not extended - against the screen that shipped, on the platform axis the epic rebuilt it around. 37 tests green across the suite's 3 spec files (8 onboarding, 3 capture, 26 flows), driving the real Tauri window against a real ephemeral core over a local HTTPS origin, on ephemeral ports, never the public network. JOURNEY 1, this machine: a fresh launch leads with what the screen is and not with the list; the picker already answers This machine - macOS; three numbered tasks read at once, the two probed ones carrying a state through the new hook and the third reading 'waiting for the first request', which is deterministic on a core nothing has reached; nothing dimmed, because nothing here waits on a precondition; the probe row present because the probes answer for this machine. Then the payoff: one proxied HTTPS request, decrypted by the core, ends setup - the lead is gone, a flow row names localhost, and the set-up banner carries 'set up another device', which is the moment-of-success sentence W5 built and the first thing the list paints. JOURNEY 2, an off-machine platform, which runs BEFORE the reveal because capture latches everProven: choosing iPhone or iPad makes reach task 1 and pushes the rest down, which is the axis claim itself; the reach task reads 'not done'; tasks 2, 3 and 4 carry data-dim=true - dimmed rather than given steps that cannot work, and still numbered, because a number is a reading order and not a claim; no probe row and no device address, because nothing here could read a phone and there is no address until reach is armed. Then answering the picker again restores this machine's own three tasks, nothing dimmed and the probe row back - the axis is reversible, which no unit test of either side observes. Plus the no-wizard case, widened: no prev/next pair, and no step-body, because the accordion retired with the stepper. THE COVERAGE MAP over the 8 assertions the old spec held, per the discipline E-DFLOW.W11 established - five succeeded (the fresh-launch pair, the three step-*-status ids, the decrypted-flow reveal, now extended with the banner), one kept verbatim (the list's absence, asserted through [aria-label=host]), and two RETIRED WITH THEIR REASON on the record rather than silently: the click-a-header-to-open case and the one-body-at-a-time case are the same accordion, and a task is always open now - what collapses is a guide, whose at-most-one-open rule is a unit invariant over all eight platforms, where every platform can be reached and an e2e can reach two. WHAT JOURNEY 2 DOES NOT DRIVE, stated in the spec's own prose rather than left to be discovered: arming reach is not reachable from a spec, for three independent reasons - confirm_reach opens a native message dialog WebDriver cannot address from inside the WKWebView, App reads reach once on mount and re-reads it only from the two calls that change it (so an out-of-band POST /reach binds a listener the window never learns of), and the suite runs one core and one window with no second launch that could come up already armed. Stubbing the confirm under the e2e cargo feature was refused and the refusal is recorded: that feature today pulls the wdio plugins and changes no behaviour, so a stub would be the first divergence between the e2e shell and the shipped one, and an e2e that proved arming by removing the authorization DEC-55 exists to require would be proving the wrong thing. The armed rendering stays where it already is, at integration and unit. TWO DEFECTS THE RUN ITSELF FOUND, neither in this WU's subject and both fixed here because the epic owes a green suite. First, the harness had not booted since pnpm 11: pnpm stopped reading the `pnpm` field in package.json and says so only as a warning, so the @wdio/native-utils 2.5.0 override was ignored, 2.4.0 resolved, and @wdio/tauri-service failed to initialise on a missing export before any spec ran - the pin moves to pnpm-workspace.yaml where pnpm 10+ reads it, and no other package.json in the tree carries a `pnpm` field. Second, flows.spec.ts's core-silence case waited for the band to EXIST; the band is one slot with a precedence rule and W5's success banner now occupies it, so the wait returned instantly and read the good news instead of the silence. The product rule is correct and untouched - a core that stopped answering outranks the good news - and what changed is the wait, which is now for what the band says. That case is green again and the two beside it are unchanged. ROUND 1 OF THE REVIEW then took two nits off this spine and the suite was re-run green after both: the option selector dropped its `li` prefix, which pinned an element Select's TSDoc does not promise and which its sibling spec already matches without a tag; and the pnpm comment, which named one version boundary where there are two and got the load-bearing one wrong - overrides are read from pnpm-workspace.yaml since 10.6, and what stopped reading the `pnpm` field in package.json is 11. The note on the previous tip stated that correctly while the comment beside the code did not, which is the wrong way round for a fact a reader meets in the file. ROUND 2 then found that fix had replaced one wrong version boundary with another - the real one is 10.5.1, established behaviourally by running eight published pnpm tarballs against two fixtures, where 10.5.0 has the machinery but an empty overrides synthesized from package.json clobbers the workspace value and 10.5.1 adds the guard. Rather than write a third version number the comment now drops the claim: its job is to say why the pin lives in this file, and the load-bearing half - that package.json's `pnpm` field is no longer read - is something pnpm prints at every install, while the minor version decides nothing here, cannot be checked from inside the repo, and had been got wrong twice. The suite is green at the tip across all three rounds." }
